    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d243">

                <pb facs="0382" n="306" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg306"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.20/749: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dodd</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-20T18:00:00+01:00">March 20, 1935—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 20—2:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>51. For the President. Am sending information in tomorrow’s pouch which I
                    promised on February 6th.</p>
                <p>I might add that in spite of Hitler’s citing Wilson’s 14 points and <pb
                        facs="0383" n="307" xml:id="pg307"/> other reassertions of peace motives, no
                    informed persons here think the one objective which I described to you has been
                    changed. With controlled thinking and increased influence Hitler may turn public
                    opinion in any direction. Anything may happen but pending crystallization allied
                    attitude this Government continues its peace propaganda. The man in the street
                    expects anything although he has little basis for independent thought.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dodd</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
